kmod: load modules through the Fil-C runtime's finit_module

libkmod's finit_module() wrapper calls syscall(__NR_finit_module), and
Fil-C's syscall() stops the program on a number it does not support
("unsupported syscall: 313"). kmod takes that path whenever a module is
uncompressed or uses the kernel's own compression. Nixpkgs 26.11's kernel
sets CONFIG_MODULE_DECOMPRESS for its .ko.xz modules (26.05's did not),
so systemd 262 as PID 1 in the initrd stopped while loading autofs4. Under Fil-C the wrapper calls zsys_finit_module(), the runtime's
implementation of the same system call.

--- a/shared/missing.h
+++ b/shared/missing.h
@@ -25,9 +25,17 @@
 
 #ifndef HAVE_FINIT_MODULE
 #include <errno.h>
+#ifdef __FILC__
+#include <pizlonated_syscalls.h>
+#endif
 
 static inline int finit_module(int fd, const char *uargs, int flags)
 {
+#ifdef __FILC__
+	/* Fil-C's syscall() stops the program on a number it does not know;
+	 * the runtime makes this one itself. */
+	return zsys_finit_module(fd, uargs, flags);
+#endif
 	if (__NR_finit_module == -1) {
 		errno = ENOSYS;
 		return -1;
